  <!-- Section 2: Orion Constellation Facts -->
  <div class="section" id="orion-facts">
    <h2>The Orion Constellation: Hunter of the Night Sky</h2>
    <p>
      Orion is one of the most famous constellations in the night sky, and it has captivated humans for thousands of years. It appears in the mythology of many cultures, from the ancient Greeks to the indigenous peoples of Australia and the Americas.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Greek mythology, Orion was a mighty hunter who was placed in the stars by Zeus. His story varies across different versions of the myth, but he is often associated with tragedy, love, and the eternal struggle between life and death. The scorpion that killed Orion was placed in the sky as the constellation Scorpius — when Scorpius rises, Orion sets, forever chased across the night sky.
    </p>
    <p>
      The stars of Orion have been used for navigation by sailors for centuries. The three stars of Orion’s Belt are particularly important because they form a straight line that points to Sirius, the brightest star in the sky. Ancient Egyptians aligned the pyramids at Giza with Orion’s Belt, believing the constellation was a gateway to the afterlife.
    </p>
    <p>
      In modern astronomy, Orion is a treasure trove of discoveries. The **Orion Nebula (Messier 42)**, located below Orion’s Belt, is a massive stellar nursery where new stars are born. It’s one of the most studied regions of space, offering scientists insights into how stars and planets form.
    </p>
    <p>
      Did you know? **NASAs Artemis missions** to return humans to the Moon are named after Artemis, Orion’s hunting partner and sister in Greek mythology. The Orion spacecraft, which will take astronauts beyond Earth orbit, is named after this legendary constellation.
    </p>
  </div>

